t the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.05$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1.05$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4b/4bcb5580c38573cfdd9839a9d305deec877945a2206aea7db6d69ec37d20ee33.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/07/07d13106e7599d36636da2101ac0bc8fb73b26062416f915a0253097eb85108c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.05$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1.05$ | $2.19$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4b/4bcb5580c38573cfdd9839a9d305deec877945a2206aea7db6d69ec37d20ee33.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)|\\le 1$ but ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{diverges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\t