[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ y={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.54$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.54$ | $0.59$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/30/30d1e296e7c83fa8925feb116c9035de14790e89ddad37ab44710feacadf13a8.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)|\\le 1,$ $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)|\\le 1,$ and ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{converges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/05/054b319170c634494c703d9588419cb6e44414196c970ca0c39689bc62f90d97.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4cb68809-3321-57e6-b36d-9022a2bbd2a5" "answer-field-68aad587-eb1a-5a9f-a4f2-2d8a2aacfd47" "answer-field-93a4c445-6329-5e2c-a9f0-b836ef949d10" "answer-field-005c8a3e-ea06-5428-9cc5-7969138abf4d" "answer-field-eeeb38c4-8164-50a5-83e3-05ef45c4cfbe" "answer-field-80ea872a-1897-55e2-913e-5854f8467ef6" "answer-field-23e492a3-3485-57dc-beff-b3d2a82c3dc4" "answer-field-209e6831-37d7-5c0c-80f8-810575f50442" "answer-field-1a7caf5e-460b-51bc-8be7-dfcc0e486b4e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4cb68809-3321-57e6-b36d-9022a2bbd2