-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty ,$ we require $a>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4fe80298-0675-5af5-b570-4bf66d34c7f6" "answer-field-c91fa969-5074-53a1-a6a0-0e61166f4954" "answer-field-208cfcf7-50ee-55e0-987f-01b27e5e3158" "answer-field-88a12a5d-43e3-53a4-a6db-7000e8813682" "answer-field-05b3f785-0733-5d37-8e6a-a7c0540c6703" "answer-field-5ee4c6ec-c6af-5c90-859f-b0dde5311d48"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4fe80298-0675-5af5-b570-4bf66d34c7f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe80298-0675-5af5-b570-4bf66d34c7f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1a95e014-2ce1-5051-a5ea-a3aa02eb1809" :answer-field/c